# Explore the Divisibility Staircase

Begin with the visual paradox: increasing the exponent makes the ideal *smaller*. Move the first slider and watch the constant witness $2^n$ survive exactly through level $n$.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The nested geometry is summarized by a direct visualization.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal the definition and strict-descent argument</strong></summary>

For $J_n=\{p:2^n\mid p(z)	ext{ for every integer }z\}$, divisibility by $2^{n+1}$ implies divisibility by $2^n$, hence $J_{n+1}\subseteq J_n$. The constant polynomial $2^n$ belongs to $J_n$ but not $J_{n+1}$, proving strictness.

See the [definition of an ideal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ideal_(ring_theory)) and the background on [integer-valued polynomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer-valued_polynomial).
</details>

## Compute binary depth

The next algorithm counts factors of two. It is the computational shadow of the filtration.

{{algorithm:0}}
{{demo:0}}
{{visualization:1}}

## Put a nonconstant polynomial under the microscope

The polynomial $ inom{x}{2}=x(x-1)/2$ is integer-valued despite its rational coefficient. Scaling it by $2^r$ places it at exact depth $r$, because its value at $x=2$ is $2^r$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}
{{demo:1}}
{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal why the infinite intersection is zero</strong></summary>

If one integer is divisible by every power of two, choose $n$ with $2^n$ larger than its absolute value; a nonzero multiple of $2^n$ cannot be that small. Thus a polynomial in every $J_n$ vanishes at every integer. A nonzero polynomial has only finitely many roots, so the polynomial is zero.
</details>

## Check the arrow before naming the chain

Use the direction laboratory to test the two implications.

{{interactive_demo:2}}
{{algorithm:1}}
{{demo:2}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal the Noetherian obstruction</strong></summary>

A [Noetherian ring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noetherian_ring) is one in which every ascending ideal chain stabilizes. Therefore no such ring supports strict ascent forever. Finite-variable polynomial rings over fields are Noetherian by the [Hilbert basis theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert%27s_basis_theorem). Their [Krull dimension](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krull_dimension) concerns prime-ideal chains and must not be confused with an infinite chain of arbitrary ideals.
</details>

## Generate exact witnesses

No search is necessary: the canonical certificate at level $n$ is the constant $2^n$.

